Fresnel-Wire
Ingredients: POST-PUNK + HAUNTING, SPOOKY & GHOSTLY + Vocoder
Creative Catalyst: Its pioneers were lighthouse keepers broadcasting to ships through long polar nights.

Fresnel-Wire is a bleak, spectral branch of post-punk that pairs the propulsive, motorik discipline of late-1970s coldwave with the uncanny articulation of vintage military vocoders. It sounds like a maritime distress beacon trapped in a cavernous radio tower: brittle, chorus-drenched basslines carve through sheets of white noise while roboticized, harmonized human speech intones nautical coordinates and existential warnings, suspended between freezing salt air and electrical failure.

Genre DNA
- Tempo & Pulse: 115–130 BPM; unyielding, stiff sixteenth-note hi-hat patterns over metronomic, driving kick-snare pulses, evoking both rotating Fresnel light gears and diesel lighthouse generators.
- Harmonic Palette: Cold modal minor tonalities (chiefly Locrian and Dorian), dominated by root-fifth bass drone movement, minor second dissonances, and flanged minor ninth chords.
- Production Signatures: Speech-tracking vocoders pushed through narrow bandpass telephone filters; high-feedback spring reverbs mimicking iron stairwells; jagged, dry direct-input electric bass counterbalanced against tape-warped guitar hiss.
- Dynamics: Songs start with isolated mechanical clicks and an uncomfortably dry vocoder whisper, slowly layering interlocking bass ostinatos and high-register guitar jangle until collapsing abruptly into the ambient thrum of sea wind and motor hum.

Scene & Origin Lore
The style germinated during the winter of 1980 along Norway's outer Helgeland coast. Isolated keepers at remote stations like Skomvær and Myken—enduring months of continuous polar night—began splicing government-issued phonetic speech synthesizers and Vocoder units into emergency shortwave transmitters. Over long nocturnal watches, they broadcast fractured post-punk bass recordings and eerie maritime advisories directly across marine VHF bands to cargo ships navigating ice floes. By 1983, tape-traders in coastal port towns like Bodø and Bergen fetishized the broadcasts, giving rise to a clandestine subculture known as "The Watch." Adherents dressed in oilskin coats smeared with graphite, heavy wool fishermen’s sweaters, and industrial surplus headphones, gathering in decommissioned slipways to dance rigidly to looped diesel pulses and spectral vocoder chants.

Typical Instruments Used:
- Roland SVC-350 and EMS Vocoder 2000
- Wal and Rickenbacker 4001 bass guitars (played with heavy metal picks through Roland JC-120 chorus amps)
- Roland CR-78 and Boss DR-55 drum machines
- Fender Jaguar (strung with flatwounds, run through dry tape echoes)
- Korg MS-20 (generating sub-bass foghorn pulses and white-noise surf)

